**WI-divin is refuted and closed `wont_do` the same day I filed it.** Asked to tackle it, I checked its premise against the shipped code first, and the premise is false.

## What the item claimed, and what the code does

WI-divin: "a `calls` edge is deduplicated by `(src, dst)` with the LINE discarded" — so collapsed call sites are unrecoverable, edge counts undercount by an unknown factor, and a consumer reading `line` "has no way to know" it is one arbitrary occurrence.

`ir.deduplicate_edges` already does the opposite, and says so in bold in its own docstring: **"The collapsed call sites are preserved, not discarded."** The survivor gains `meta["call_lines"]`, the sorted union of every collapsed site. `_absorb_call_site` unions the lines *and* every registry-declared per-call-site meta key (`per_call_site_keys()`) — a discipline INV-vukiv already paid for. And the ABSENCE of the key is a documented contract meaning "exactly one site, at `edge.line`" — which is precisely the "way to know" the item asserted did not exist. There are 59 references to it across five core test files.

Measured on the very survey the item was filed from (pretix, arm B):

    18,075 of 76,635 `calls` edges carry meta["call_lines"]
    59,806 sites recorded — 41,731 beyond their survivors

The information I claimed was destroyed was sitting in the file I was reading.

## So the defect was the instrument, and the corrected number is better

`diffsurvey.py` keyed on `edge.line` and never read `call_lines`. Re-derived through the documented field:

    django.db.models CALL SITES   B 10,422 -> S 11,869   NEW 1,447   GONE 0

against the **+756 pairs / "110 apparent removals"** measurement 0022 published. The corrected edge-level result is **nearly double the published gain, with no removals to explain at all** — the "110 disappeared edges" never existed as a phenomenon.

Corrected in three places: the measurement record's Result and its confound item 1, the measurements README row, and the CHANGELOG entry. Retracted text is **replaced, not struck through**, per the standing rule.

The one genuine bound is `_CALL_LINES_CAP = 50`, which 36 pretix edges reach and which `ir.py` documents at the point it applies. It does not resurrect the item.

## What I should have done

LIVE rule 1: the fix may already exist — grep the shipped code **and the target file's own header** before filing. The answer was in the docstring of the function I was theorising about. Rule 2 is the companion and names this exact failure: an item is usually right that something is broken and wrong about which part, *including an item I filed hours ago*.

## Residual filed, scoped correctly this time

**WI-jigov** — an edge locates to a LINE, not a column, so two calls with the same callee NAME on one line are indistinguishable to a consumer joining an edge back to source. Different mechanism from dedup; it is what actually bit two of my instruments today (5 of INV-tanom's first 6 hits, and a share of 0022's 1,677 failed line/name joins). The item states plainly that this may be working as intended — a line is the natural granularity for the primary consumers — and that adding a column is a schema change needing its own sizing, held to the same discipline as INV-tanom. It also carries an explicit instruction not to close it by pointing at `call_lines`, since conflating the two is what made WI-divin wrong.

Docs only; no behaviour change.

